    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $f'(x)$ for the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^3}{3}+x^2+5x+7$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=x^2+2x+5$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{1}{3}(3x^2)+2x+5\\\\f'(x)&=x^2+2x+5\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By completing the square, show that $f'(x)$ is always positive, and hence that $f(x)$ is increasing for all $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=(x+1)^2+4\\geq4>0$, so $f(x)$ is increasing for all $x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=x^2+2x+5\\\\f'(x)&=(x+1)^2-1+5\\\\f'(x)&=(x+1)^2+4\\end{align*}$$\nSince $(x+1)^2\\geq0$, we have $f'(x)\\geq4>0$ for all $x$. Therefore $f(x)$ is increasing for all $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Explain why the equation $f(x)=0$ has exactly one real solution.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is continuous, with $f(-2)=-\\dfrac53<0<f(0)=7$, so it has a root between $-2$ and $0$. Since $f$ is strictly increasing, this is its only real root.",
          "worked": "Since $f'(x)>0$ for all $x$, $f$ is strictly increasing and can have at most one real root.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-2)&=\\frac{(-2)^3}{3}+(-2)^2+5(-2)+7\\\\f(-2)&=-\\frac53<0\\\\f(0)&=7>0\\end{align*}$$\nAs a polynomial, $f$ is continuous, so its graph crosses the $x$-axis between $-2$ and $0$. Therefore $f(x)=0$ has exactly one real solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      }
    ]
  },
